    {
      "id": "3422b77e-b3dd-4cf5-9133-3536bd22d373",
      "unit_id": "da53107f-5c43-4b13-8d6c-bf5f5465f5ec",
      "type": "reading",
      "title": "New reading",
      "content": "<p>The Importance of Sleep Sleep is one of the most essential biological functions for human beings. Although modern society often treats sleep as less important than work or study, scientists have repeatedly demonstrated that insufficient sleep negatively affects memory, concentration, emotional stability, and physical health. Adults generally require between seven and nine hours of sleep each night, while teenagers and children need even more due to their ongoing physical and mental development. During sleep, the brain processes information collected throughout the day. Researchers explain that memories become stronger because the brain reorganizes and stores important details while sleeping. This is one reason why students who sleep properly often perform better on exams compared to those who stay awake all night studying. Sleep also helps regulate emotions. People who are sleep deprived may become more anxious, impatient, or emotionally sensitive. In addition to supporting mental health, sleep plays a critical role in maintaining physical health. During deep sleep, the body repairs muscles, strengthens the immune system, and releases hormones necessary for growth and recovery. Several medical studies have shown that long-term sleep deprivation increases the risk of heart disease, diabetes, obesity, and depression. Experts therefore recommend maintaining a regular sleep schedule and limiting screen time before bed. However, achieving healthy sleep patterns has become increasingly difficult in the digital age. Smartphones, laptops, and social media platforms encourage people to remain mentally active late into the night. Blue light emitted from electronic screens can interfere with the production of melatonin, the hormone responsible for helping people fall asleep. As a result, many individuals experience insomnia or poor-quality sleep without fully understanding the cause. Some companies and schools have started recognizing the importance of sleep and are adjusting schedules to support healthier lifestyles. Certain workplaces now allow flexible starting hours, while some educational institutions begin classes later in the morning to better match students’ natural sleep cycles. Researchers believe these changes may improve productivity, academic performance, and overall wellbeing in the future.</p>",
